Z||000014||FieroMontreal||12-11-2001||08:57 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by SS_304:<BR><B>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> <P>As the owner of a '91 CRX HF (in the shop right now for CV joint replacement), I give my condolences.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>HF?? Tisk Tisk Tisk... Did you not know about Si?<P><P>------------------<BR> -Alex <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigfieromontrealholidays.gif"> <BR>1986 Fiero GT, STOCK Wingless, <BR>President of FieroMontreal... <A HREF="http://www.fieromontreal.com" TARGET=_blank>www.fieromontreal.com</A> <BR>and Regional Director of CFOG-I for Eastern Canada.||||reg||1
Z||000015||rogergarrison||12-12-2001||07:51 AM||||Not sure about there, but pretty sure the insurance company is OBLIGATED to pay the balance of a note to lender. If they didnt, I sure wouldnt pay any more of the payments, let the lender sue the insurance company. Get a lawyer to be sure. The dealer I do repairs for on repos, sues the buyers/ insurance companies all the time to collect. Biggest example is the guy that gets into a big wreck, takes the insurance check for himself and lets them repo whats left of the car. He takes the insurance company to court since by law, there supposed to make checks payable to the lien holder as a co-payee. If they dont, just like to the owner and body shop and the cars not repaired, they force the insurance company to pay again to them. They do that about 2 times a month, and everytime the insurance company has to pay again. ||||reg||1
Z||000016||thomas_l||12-12-2001||10:00 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rogergarrison:<BR><B>Not sure about there, but pretty sure the insurance company is OBLIGATED to pay the balance of a note to lender. </B><P><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That may be true in some states, but most insurance covers the car not the loan. Most car loans or leases will offer a "gap" policy that gets rolled into the monthly payments. It's only purpose is to cover the gap between the car's value and the amount owed. I have that on both of my newer cars and it amounts to something like $2 to $3 a month. So for something like $70/year I know that if either one gets totalled I walk away debt free.<P>Another way to do it is get the car appraised at the higher value and make sure you have a policy that supports it.<P>Or roll the dice and hope you never end up with a total loss and a payment left over.||||reg||1
Z||000017||BlackStang95||12-12-2001||10:26 AM||||I'm with mach10, i don't like the car but it sucks it got stolen.  I gotta wonder why anyone would steal that car...not just because i dont see the appeal but wouldn't it be conspicious as hell?   A neon green riced crx sure stands out compared to a champagne grey accord.  The only thing I can think of is they are ricers too and want the custom parts.  <P>If theres anything worse than a ricer, its gotta be a ricer thief!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif">||||reg||1
